  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The optimization model and trade-off analyses are evaluated using data from a single pilot study in Stockholm, which may limit direct applicability to cities with different waterway geographies or waste infrastructure.",
      "what": "This study models a water-bound recyclable waste collection system using a mobile barge and temporary quay assembly points, testing it with real-world pilot data from Stockholm. A two-stage mixed-integer linear programming model was developed to maximize service coverage while minimizing routing emissions across water transport, transshipment points, and road transport to recycling centers. The analysis evaluates how visit spacing policies and increased barge storage capacities affect the trade-offs between road and water emissions and social accessibility.",
      "why": "It establishes a quantitative framework for evaluating the environmental and social trade-offs of urban waterway logistics, demonstrating that while transshipment points increase water-based emissions, they can reduce overall negative externalities by cutting down road transport trips."
    }
